<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    Two layers: chrome, and the stage.

    The chrome is a title bar over one Frame, with no NavigationView. A NavigationView disambiguates five or
    more peer destinations, and Ripcord has one destination and two utilities — so its steady state would be
    a column whose only job is to highlight the page you are already on. On a 1280px handheld that column is
    most of a fourth console card, and it would make the pane the first focus stop instead of the thing a
    gamer came to play.

    The stage is a separate Frame over the chrome: a running stream is not a place you navigate to inside
    app furniture — it owns the window, it is never in a back stack, and GoBack cannot reach it. Hosting it
    inside a control's content area would also inherit that control's border and rounded corner, a thin
    light line along the top and left of the video.
-->
<Window
    x:Class="Ripcord_App.MainWindow"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:controls="using:Ripcord_App.Controls"
    xmlns:local="using:Ripcord_App"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    Title="Ripcord"
    mc:Ignorable="d">
    <Window.SystemBackdrop>
        <MicaBackdrop />
    </Window.SystemBackdrop>

    <!--
        XYFocusKeyboardNavigation gives the ARROW KEYS the same directional model the pad already uses, for
        free — it only applies when the focused element does not handle the arrow itself, so text boxes,
        sliders and scrollers keep their own behaviour untouched.

        The point is not the keyboard feature. It is that the pad's Down and the keyboard's Down land on the
        same element. Two directional models over one layout is how a surface ends up navigable with one
        input and subtly wrong with the other, and nobody notices until someone puts a keyboard down and picks
        a controller up.

        A plain arrow that reaches this grid unhandled goes through FocusPilot, the pad's own path, because
        the pad has a straight-line fallback the focus engine does not (see OnRootKeyDown). This setting
        covers what does not bubble here.
    -->
    <Grid XYFocusKeyboardNavigation="Enabled">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <!--  Set to 0 from code while a stream runs full screen; a windowed stream keeps the title bar.  -->
            <RowDefinition x:Name="TitleBarRow" Height="48" />
            <RowDefinition Height="*" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <TitleBar
            x:Name="AppTitleBar"
            Title="Ripcord"
            BackRequested="TitleBar_BackRequested"
            IsBackButtonVisible="{x:Bind ChromeFrame.CanGoBack, Mode=OneWay}">
            <TitleBar.IconSource>
                <ImageIconSource ImageSource="Assets/AppIcon.ico" />
            </TitleBar.IconSource>

            <!--
                The two utilities, as commands rather than destinations — which is what they are. Each is one
                focus stop with an access key, so they are reachable by pad, by Tab and by Alt, and neither
                costs a permanent column of the window.

                Compact touch target rather than the full 48: the bar is 48 tall, so a 48 button fills it edge
                to edge. This is the one place the minimum is knowingly not met, which is what the separate
                token is for.
            -->
            <TitleBar.RightHeader>
                <StackPanel
                    x:Name="TitleBarCommands"
                    Orientation="Horizontal"
                    Spacing="2"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <Button
                        x:Name="SettingsButton"
                        MinWidth="{StaticResource RipcordTouchTargetCompact}"
                        MinHeight="{StaticResource RipcordTouchTargetCompact}"
                        AccessKey="S"
                        x:Uid="MainWindow_Settings"
                        Click="OnSettingsClick"
                        Style="{StaticResource SubtleButtonStyle}">
                        <FontIcon FontSize="{ThemeResource RipcordIconSizeAction}" Glyph="&#xE713;" />
                    </Button>
                    <Button
                        x:Name="AboutButton"
                        MinWidth="{StaticResource RipcordTouchTargetCompact}"
                        MinHeight="{StaticResource RipcordTouchTargetCompact}"
                        AccessKey="A"
                        x:Uid="MainWindow_AboutRipcord"
                        Click="OnAboutClick"
                        Style="{StaticResource SubtleButtonStyle}">
                        <FontIcon FontSize="{ThemeResource RipcordIconSizeAction}" Glyph="&#xE946;" />
                    </Button>
                </StackPanel>
            </TitleBar.RightHeader>
        </TitleBar>

        <Frame x:Name="ChromeFrame" Grid.Row="1" />

        <Frame
            x:Name="StreamFrame"
            Grid.Row="1"
            Background="Black"
            Visibility="Collapsed" />
        <!--
            The prompts sit over whatever is in row 1 rather than taking a row of their own: they come and go
            with the input mode, and a row that collapses would reflow the page under the user every time they
            put a pad down. Over the stream layer too, deliberately — a Modal scope pushed mid-session is
            exactly when someone needs telling which button answers it.

            What they would cover lifts clear of them instead (PushBottomInset): the stream's bottom band, and
            the chrome pages' bottom edge. So a page's bottom does move while the prompts show, by their height.
        -->
        <controls:InputHintBar
            x:Name="HintBar"
            Grid.Row="1"
            VerticalAlignment="Bottom" />
    </Grid>
</Window>
